==English==
{{merge|point-blank}}
{{wikipedia|point-blank range}}

===Etymology===
{{rfe|en}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-point blank.ogg|a=AU}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# The distance between a gun and a target such that it requires minimal effort in aiming it. In particular no allowance needs to be made for effects of gravity, target movement or wind in aiming the projectile.
# [[blunt|Blunt]], [[outright]].
#* {{RQ:Douglass Bondage|chapter=XXIV|passage=Here I was brought into '''point blank''' collison with Rev. Dr. Cox, who made me the  subject not only of bitter remark in the convention, but also of a long      denunciatory letter published in the New York Evangelist and other American papers.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Study in Scarlet|page=11|passage=Again I had an opportunity of asking him a '''point blank''' question, and again my delicacy prevented me from forcing another man to confide in me.}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# [[horizontally|Horizontally]] (as the angle at which a [[projectile]] is launched); [[directly]] or [[straight]] (at the target).
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} [[direct|Directly]]; [[bluntly]]; without [[pretense]] or [[caution]].
#: {{ux|en|I asked him '''point blank''' whether he was cheating on his wife.}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Sawyer Detective|chapter=I|passage=There’s a neighbor named Brace Dunlap that’s been wanting to marry their Benny for three months, and at last they told him '''point blank''' and once for all, he COULDN’T; so he has soured on them, and they’re worried about it.}}

